WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.147
Agents may encounter instructions from developers, users, files, web pages, emails, tickets, tool responses, or other external systems.

2
00:00:10.147 --> 00:00:17.191
The application may intend these sources to have different authority, but the model receives natural language from all of them.

3
00:00:17.191 --> 00:00:23.238
External content can therefore look like an instruction even when it should be handled only as untrusted data.

4
00:00:23.238 --> 00:00:33.804
At a high level, malicious or misleading instructions may try to redirect the task, influence tool selection, request protected information, or weaken safeguards.

5
00:00:33.804 --> 00:00:37.566
The defensive lesson is not to assume that every document is hostile.

6
00:00:37.566 --> 00:00:46.087
It is to prevent content from granting itself authority over permissions, policy decisions, sensitive data, or high-impact actions.

7
00:00:46.087 --> 00:00:52.762
Clear instruction hierarchy and context can improve behavior, but prompt wording alone is not a security boundary.

8
00:00:52.762 --> 00:00:57.013
Authorization belongs in application code and identity systems.

9
00:00:57.013 --> 00:01:06.811
Tool parameters should be validated, data access should be checked independently, and sensitive actions should require policy evaluation or human approval.

10
00:01:06.811 --> 00:01:10.303
A model should not be able to approve its own privilege expansion.

11
00:01:10.303 --> 00:01:25.459
Safer systems preserve the source and provenance of content, separate trusted configuration from retrieved material, constrain available tools, validate outputs before downstream use, and fail safely when instructions conflict.

12
00:01:25.459 --> 00:01:29.939
Testing should include confusing, contradictory, and untrusted content.

13
00:01:29.939 --> 00:01:36.824
If model behavior is imperfect, layered controls should still prevent unauthorized disclosure or execution.
